Track 2: Szum
- Story & Context: Tomasz wrote this character portrait about an aging technician at the Łódź automatons lab who spent his night shifts re-tuning broken robot actuators to play warm pentatonic intervals. The track relies entirely on a muted alto sax playing against a soft, hand-clapped indie-pop beat recorded inside a concrete stairwell. During the final chorus, the saxophone pitch drops an entire octave, mimicking a fading battery on a vintage tape player. The haunting vocal hook whispers "I heard your gear-heart slipping through the floorboard seams" before fading into thirty seconds of pure vinyl hiss.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Dreamy, soft-focus artwork featuring an old analog tape deck sitting on a windowsill covered in dried lavender, overlooking a foggy post-industrial skyline. Pale blue and amber lighting, poetic atmospheric light, hyper-detailed indie aesthetic.

Track 3: Did We Cross The Ridge Line Yet?
- Story & Context: The final track serves as the scene's ultimate myth, detailing the fictional escape of three prototype chassis into the Karkonosze mountains during a mid-winter blizzard. Built around a driving, 808-style indie-pop bassline and soaring, multi-layered sax melodies, the song contrasts high-energy rhythmic pop with vast, freezing spatial reverb. The climax features forty stacked vocal tracks singing in unison, creating an ethereal wall of sound that suddenly cuts to an acoustic saxophone breath. The last lyrics echoed through the cavernous mix are "Leave our serial numbers in the melting drift."
